.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt QUARK_GET_BOOTTIME 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm quark_get_boottime
.Nd get the boottime epoch for wallclock translation
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In quark.h
.Ft u64
.Fn quark_get_boottime "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
returns the boottime epoch: the wallclock time of boot in nanoseconds
since the Unix epoch, computed as the difference of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
and
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME .
This is the same value the kernel exposes as the btime field of
.Pa /proc/stat ,
but in nanosecond instead of one second resolution.
.Pp
Adding the epoch to a nanoseconds-since-boot timestamp yields
nanoseconds since the Unix epoch:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
u64 epoch = quark_get_boottime();

for each event
	wallclock = event_time + epoch;
.Ed
.Pp
Times since boot are immune to system clock changes, wallclock times
are not: translate at the last moment, when a time leaves the system
for display or storage.
Gradual clock corrections (slewing) adjust the wallclock and the boot
clock at the same rate, leaving the epoch untouched, only a step moves
it, as when NTP corrects a clock that was wrong at boot.
.Pp
The epoch is sampled at most every 10ms, regardless of how frequently
this function gets called, and a new sample is only adopted when it
strays more than 10ms from the previously adopted one: only an actual
clock step moves the epoch that much, anything below is sampling noise
that would jitter conversions from call to call.
Successive calls return the same value absent a clock step.
A call costs little more than a clock read, fetching it once per batch
of events is the recommended pattern, and the Go bindings depend on
it to avoid a cgo call per event.
.Pp
.Nm
may be called from any thread without synchronization.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The boottime epoch in nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.
Zero is returned if the epoch could not be sampled, which should not
happen on a functioning system.
